    "facts": "",
    "text": "Research · System design How to choose a system boundary Draw the line where state is committed, work is handed off and failures can be retried safely. TK Tony Koo 15 October 2026 · 3 min read Source-backed editorial synthesis Separate the decision from the side effect A synchronous call is easiest when the caller needs an immediate answer and the operation is short. A queue helps when work is slow, bursty or should continue after the caller disconnects. Neither removes failure. First decide which system owns the authoritative state, which actions can repeat, and what the caller sees when the outcome is uncertain. Figure. The order and its event commit together; a worker publishes the event later. Consumers still need duplicate handling. Source: Microsoft Azure Architecture Center · Outbox sequence ( CC BY 4.0 ). Suppose an approval service records “approved” and then sends a notification. If the database succeeds but sending fails, the two systems disagree. If sending succeeds first but the transaction rolls back, the message lies. Commit state and intent together The transactional outbox pattern writes business state and an event row in the same database transaction. A separate worker publishes the event. This avoids a dual-write gap, but delivery may still repeat. The consumer needs an idempotency key or processed-message record so retries do not duplicate effects. Worked example An approval request carries ID REQ-42 . The service commits the approval and outbox event together. A worker publishes it twice after a timeout; the notification consumer recognizes REQ-42 and sends once. Specify the uncertain outcome Every boundary needs a recovery story: timeout, duplicate, partial work, and poison message. Use durable state and retry limits. If an external action cannot be rolled back, define a compensating action or human escalation rather than calling a timeout “failure” when the effect may already have happened. Read the original sources AWS transactional outbox The dual-write failure and idempotent consumer design. PostgreSQL transactions Atomic transaction behavior for the local commit. Microsoft Azure Architecture Center · Outbox sequence MicrosoftDocs/architecture-center diagram rendered from SVG; bottom publisher logo omitted from the crop. Read next: What the cloud should own In this story"
